1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,780
Hi guys!

2
00:00:01,780 --> 00:00:07,750
Today it's "Annyeong" instead of "Annyeong-haseyo".

3
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:09,610
Hi

4
00:00:09,610 --> 00:00:17,630
Today, I'm not using formal language; I'm using informal language.

5
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:18,940
Informal language

6
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,610
Are you surprised?

7
00:00:20,610 --> 00:00:22,730
Are you really surprised?

8
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:29,380
But why am I suddenly speaking informally today?

9
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:32,960
Does anyone know why?

10
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:37,150
I'll give you a hint.

11
00:00:37,150 --> 00:00:44,220
It was April 1st recently.

12
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:49,310
April 1st

13
00:00:49,310 --> 00:00:51,540
What day is it?

14
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:54,370
It's April Fools' Day!

15
00:00:54,370 --> 00:00:58,850
In Korean, we call it "Manujeol".

16
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:03,260
People play a lot of pranks on April Fools' Day.

17
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:07,200
And they tell a lot of lies.

18
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,970
Lies

19
00:01:08,970 --> 00:01:14,240
That's why I'm speaking informally today.

20
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,900
I'm only going to do it for today.

21
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:19,550
Only today

22
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:23,470
I'll speak informally just for today.

23
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:29,060
Some people call me "Teacher".

24
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:30,740
Teacher Ria

25
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:36,990
But today, let's just talk like we're friends.

26
00:01:36,990 --> 00:01:41,060
Let's use informal language like close friends.

27
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:42,470
Got it?

28
00:01:42,470 --> 00:01:47,910
In Korea, there's something called "Yaja Time".

29
00:01:47,910 --> 00:01:49,500
Yaja Time

30
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:51,480
It's the name of a game.

31
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:52,450
Yaja Time

32
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:57,520
Normally, you use formal language when you first meet.

33
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:07,490
And younger people use formal language to older people.

34
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:15,180
Older people use informal language to younger people.

35
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:21,570
But in "Yaja Time", this is reversed.

36
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:22,820
Opposite

37
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:28,900
For example, I would use informal language to a professor.

38
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:35,650
To a professor: "Hey Professor, where are you going?"

39
00:02:35,650 --> 00:02:44,030
Instead of "Where are you going, Professor?", you say "Where you going?".

40
00:02:44,030 --> 00:02:45,970
It's so scary.

41
00:02:45,970 --> 00:02:50,300
Just imagining it is so scary.

42
00:02:50,300 --> 00:03:01,110
Then on the flip side, the professor says "Ria-nim, where are you going?" to me.

43
00:03:01,110 --> 00:03:03,700
That's the game.

44
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:06,140
Isn't it so fun?

45
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:13,860
Usually, when playing this game, younger people really like it.

46
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:16,660
They find it super fun.

47
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:23,030
Conversely, older people get "angry".

48
00:03:23,030 --> 00:03:24,220
Angry

49
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:26,460
Why would they be angry?

50
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:34,510
They feel offended because a younger person is speaking informally to them.

51
00:03:34,510 --> 00:03:41,120
It's like, "How dare you speak informally to me?".

52
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:49,450
But since it's a game, they aren't truly angry; it's just like a prank.

53
00:03:49,450 --> 00:03:54,420
Is there an idol you like?

54
00:03:54,420 --> 00:04:02,060
Then try searching "group name + Yaja Time" on YouTube.

55
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:06,730
You'll probably be able to find most of them.

56
00:04:06,730 --> 00:04:13,380
The easiest way to speak informally is to drop the "yo".

57
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:16,750
Ban-ga-wo-yo (Nice to meet you) - Ban-ga-wo

58
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:19,900
Bap meog-eoss-eo-yo? (Did you eat?) - Bap meog-eoss-eo?

59
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:23,650
Now you guys try practicing too.

60
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:28,590
Mwo jo-a-hae-yo? (What do you like?) - Mwo jo-a-hae?

61
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:33,920
Hang-uk-eo gong-bu-hae-yo? (Do you study Korean?) - Hang-uk-eo gong-bu-hae?

62
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:38,380
And the most important thing is the name!

63
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:40,990
It's when you call someone's name.

64
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:48,600
When using formal language, you say Ria-nim or Teacher Ria, right?

65
00:04:48,600 --> 00:05:00,350
But when speaking informally, you use "ya" like Ria-ya, or "a" if there is a bottom consonant.

66
00:05:00,350 --> 00:05:03,010
Like "Ji-eun-a".

67
00:05:03,010 --> 00:05:08,930
And when you don't want to say their name, just use "Ya".

68
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:11,260
Just say "Ya"

69
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:13,170
Hey, what are you doing?

70
00:05:13,170 --> 00:05:18,140
But this is really casual informal language.

71
00:05:18,140 --> 00:05:24,330
Usually, you use the name with "a/ya".

72
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:31,330
Do you guys like informal language or formal language when speaking Korean?

73
00:05:31,330 --> 00:05:38,300
But you might not get many chances to hear informal language.

74
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:40,880
Might not have many chances

75
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:47,150
You can hear it in K-dramas or on YouTube.

76
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:49,280
You can hear it though

77
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:55,360
But on broadcasts, they mostly use formal language.

78
00:05:55,360 --> 00:06:03,830
Usually, I use informal language a lot when I'm close or comfortable with someone.

79
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:13,920
Then, if you're the same age, do you automatically use informal language?

80
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:14,870
No.

81
00:06:14,870 --> 00:06:23,330
Even if you're the same age, you almost always use formal language when you first meet.

82
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:29,420
Of course, really young kids don't know about this.

83
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:37,170
So when they first meet, they say "Hi, what's your name?" in a cute way.

84
00:06:37,170 --> 00:06:50,070
Let me tell you about the process of how Koreans start using informal language.

85
00:06:50,070 --> 00:06:57,030
Just when and how exactly do we start speaking informally?

86
00:06:57,030 --> 00:07:03,420
First, you greet each other when you first meet.

87
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:04,890
"Annyeong-haseyo"

88
00:07:04,890 --> 00:07:09,830
Then you introduce yourself.

89
00:07:09,830 --> 00:07:16,500
You say your name... and then Koreans ask about age.

90
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:20,630
"By the way, how old are you?"

91
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:28,070
If the other person is older than me.

92
00:07:28,070 --> 00:07:36,220
Then usually the younger person says this first.

93
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:38,590
"Uh... please speak comfortably"

94
00:07:38,590 --> 00:07:40,940
Please speak comfortably

95
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:42,790
They say it like this

96
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:45,280
Please speak comfortably?

97
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:47,130
Speech is comfortable?

98
00:07:47,130 --> 00:07:49,490
What does this mean?

99
00:07:49,490 --> 00:07:53,770
Yep, that's right! it means to speak informally.

100
00:07:53,770 --> 00:08:00,290
Of course, you could just say "It's okay to speak informally".

101
00:08:00,290 --> 00:08:06,430
But we say "Please speak comfortably" really often.

102
00:08:06,430 --> 00:08:11,300
It's a very natural Korean expression.

103
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:17,310
Or, "Drop your speech."

104
00:08:17,310 --> 00:08:21,550
"You can drop your speech."

105
00:08:21,550 --> 00:08:24,840
We say it like this really often.

106
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:31,140
Drop your speech? It's the same.

107
00:08:31,140 --> 00:08:33,700
It means to speak informally.

108
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:38,850
You've just confirmed each other's ages.

109
00:08:38,850 --> 00:08:51,990
But just because I'm older, suddenly speaking informally is a bit... awkward, right?

110
00:08:51,990 --> 00:08:59,230
So usually the younger person asks first.

111
00:08:59,230 --> 00:09:00,920
Asks first

112
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:04,340
"Please speak comfortably to me."

113
00:09:04,340 --> 00:09:06,190
"You can drop your speech."

114
00:09:06,190 --> 00:09:12,820
Then how does the older person respond?

115
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:17,700
Honestly, this varies a lot from person to person.

116
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:20,480
Everyone is different

117
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:26,720
Some people drop their speech immediately.

118
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:31,410
"Sure, got it. You speak comfortably, too."

119
00:09:31,410 --> 00:09:38,230
Once that happens, you both start speaking informally.

120
00:09:38,230 --> 00:09:42,510
Or they just say "Okay, got it."

121
00:09:42,510 --> 00:09:49,570
Then the younger person keeps using formal language.

122
00:09:49,570 --> 00:09:54,270
Only the older person speaks informally to me.

123
00:09:54,270 --> 00:10:00,250
This is how we decide.

124
00:10:00,250 --> 00:10:02,070
What about you guys?

125
00:10:02,070 --> 00:10:11,200
If someone says "Please speak comfortably" or "Drop your speech" to you, will you speak informally?

126
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:21,230
Some Koreans find informal language a bit uncomfortable.

127
00:10:21,230 --> 00:10:24,770
They find it a bit awkward.

128
00:10:24,770 --> 00:10:27,050
Find it awkward

129
00:10:27,050 --> 00:10:31,810
But I love informal language!

130
00:10:31,810 --> 00:10:40,340
Because honestly, it feels like there's a wall when using formal language.

131
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:41,620
Wall

132
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:48,860
And there are so many things to be careful about with formal language.

133
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:51,980
Too many things to be careful about

134
00:10:51,980 --> 00:10:54,050
You guys know, right?

135
00:10:54,050 --> 00:10:59,570
You know how complex Korean formal language is, right?

136
00:10:59,570 --> 00:11:08,560
Especially with grandmas, grandpas, or particularly professors who are much older than me.

137
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:14,910
I'm very careful when speaking in front of a professor.

138
00:11:14,910 --> 00:11:21,490
I have a lot to say about professors, but I'll tell you about it later.

139
00:11:21,490 --> 00:11:27,170
But I'm so comfortable when speaking informally.

140
00:11:27,170 --> 00:11:34,750
And I feel like I've really become close to the person.

141
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:45,780
It varies by person, but I think you have to speak informally to be "real friends".

142
00:11:45,780 --> 00:11:55,890
Not just someone you meet at work or see once in a while, but a real friend.

143
00:11:55,890 --> 00:12:01,090
I feel our hearts getting closer.

144
00:12:01,090 --> 00:12:04,130
But I said it earlier, right?

145
00:12:04,130 --> 00:12:11,240
Some Koreans find informal language a bit awkward.

146
00:12:11,240 --> 00:12:16,110
"Are we close enough to speak informally?"

147
00:12:16,110 --> 00:12:19,620
"I don't think we're quite there yet."

148
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:21,630
That's the kind of vibe.

149
00:12:21,630 --> 00:12:27,080
Of course, people don't actually say that out loud.

150
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:31,410
But they think it to themselves.

151
00:12:31,410 --> 00:12:32,690
Inwardly

152
00:12:32,690 --> 00:12:35,870
Let me tell you my story.

153
00:12:35,870 --> 00:12:41,340
I met someone a long time ago.

154
00:12:41,340 --> 00:12:44,070
Met for the first time

155
00:12:44,070 --> 00:12:49,730
Like I said before, we told each other our ages.

156
00:12:49,730 --> 00:12:56,320
The person was three years older than me.

157
00:12:56,320 --> 00:13:03,900
So I said, "Please speak comfortably. You can drop your speech."

158
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:14,530
They replied, "It's okay. I'll drop it slowly later."

159
00:13:14,530 --> 00:13:20,790
Since I like informal language, I was a bit bummed.

160
00:13:20,790 --> 00:13:22,180
Bummed

161
00:13:22,180 --> 00:13:27,620
Anyway, everyone is different like this.

162
00:13:27,620 --> 00:13:35,540
The second reason I like informal language is... well, it's just cute.

163
00:13:35,540 --> 00:13:38,590
"Bap meog-eoss-eo-yo? Mwo meog-eoss-eo-yo?"

164
00:13:38,590 --> 00:13:41,950
Vs "Bap meog-eoss-eo? Mwo meog-eoss-eo?"

165
00:13:41,950 --> 00:13:45,380
Which one is more comfortable and cute?

166
00:13:45,380 --> 00:13:51,010
I think informal language is a bit cuter.

167
00:13:51,010 --> 00:14:01,390
I like it because when speaking informally, it sounds cuter and I feel closer and more comfortable with the person.

168
00:14:01,390 --> 00:14:10,260
Well, anyway, I did my first and last informal podcast like this today.

169
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:19,990
Or should I speak informally like this on next year's April Fools' Day too?

170
00:14:19,990 --> 00:14:23,640
If you guys like it, I'll do it.

171
00:14:23,640 --> 00:14:29,240
And this kind of casual, comfortable Korean.

172
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:40,120
This kind of Korean informal language that can be used between really close friends, boyfriends, and girlfriends!

173
00:14:40,120 --> 00:14:50,590
If you're really interested in informal language, check out my channel's membership videos.

174
00:14:50,590 --> 00:15:00,120
I occasionally post what I really want to say informally without a script.

175
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:02,340
I said it before, right?

176
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:09,200
I always speak informally with people I'm really close to.

177
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:13,700
At first, you have to learn formal Korean.

178
00:15:13,700 --> 00:15:19,330
So normally, I post formal language videos.

179
00:15:19,330 --> 00:15:24,910
Anyway, today was a podcast with an informal language concept.

180
00:15:24,910 --> 00:15:29,330
I hope you guys liked it!

181
00:15:29,330 --> 00:15:40,640
If you know how to use informal language in Korean, leave a comment in informal language.

182
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:42,500
You can say anything

183
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:44,360
Well, bye!

184
00:15:44,360 --> 00:15:55,340
See you next time! Bye :)
